            <td>18,421,800 B (17.5 MB)</td>
            <td>49.6% Reduction</td>
            <td>1,420 ms</td>
            <td>Infinity (Lossless)</td>
            <td>100% of Browsers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Apple HEIC (Source)</strong></td>
            <td>2,411,520 B (2.30 MB)</td>
            <td>86.8% Reduction</td>
            <td>Camera Hardware</td>
            <td>44.2 dB</td>
            <td>Apple Safari Only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Standard JPEG (Quality 92)</strong></td>
            <td>3,145,728 B (3.00 MB)</td>
            <td>82.9% Reduction</td>
            <td>180 ms</td>
            <td>43.8 dB</td>
            <td>100% Universal</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Google WebP (Quality 90)</strong></td>
            <td>2,198,400 B (2.09 MB)</td>
            <td>88.0% Reduction</td>
            <td>240 ms</td>
            <td>44.6 dB</td>
            <td>97.8% of Browsers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>AOMedia AVIF (Quality 85)</strong></td>
            <td>1,468,000 B (1.39 MB)</td>
            <td>92.0% Reduction</td>
            <td>620 ms</td>
            <td>45.1 dB</td>
            <td>93.5% of Browsers</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      In this real-world benchmark, converting the 17.5 MB raw PNG to an optimized AVIF file achieved a 92 percent reduction in storage volume (down to 1.39 MB) while sustaining an exceptional PSNR of 45.1 dB. For universal compatibility across legacy operating systems, converting the Apple HEIC photo to JPEG required only 180 milliseconds and yielded a universally shareable 3.0 MB file with pristine visual sharpness.
    </p>

    <h2>Vector Architecture: In-Browser Transcoding Pipeline</h2>
